# Docker reads the .dockerignore at the *context root* only. Both images now
# build from a root context (`docker build -f backend/Dockerfile .`) so the
# repo-root LICENSE, COPYRIGHT and TRADEMARKS.md are inside the context. That
# means this is the only .dockerignore Docker reads: the former
# frontend/.dockerignore stopped applying the moment the context moved, with no
# warning and no error, so its two rules were folded in here. The backend
# context grew the same way -- from ./backend to the whole repo -- so this file
# is also what keeps that build from dragging the entire working tree in.
#
# Pattern scoping: patterns are relative to the context root, so a bare
# `node_modules` matches only a *top-level* node_modules, NOT
# frontend/node_modules. Anything that can appear at depth is therefore listed
# with a `**/` prefix. Docker's `**` is documented to match zero or more path
# segments, which should make `**/x` cover a top-level `x` as well -- but we do
# not rely on that assumption: where it matters, both the bare and the `**/`
# form are listed. A redundant pattern is harmless; a missing one is a silent
# regression. Patterns match whole path components, so `build` matches a
# directory named exactly `build` and never the file backend/build.gradle.kts.

# --- Host build output (was frontend/.dockerignore) ---------------------------
# Must never enter the image build context. The frontend build's `COPY` would
# otherwise overwrite the Alpine-installed node_modules with Windows-native
# binaries (notably lightningcss-win32-x64-msvc), breaking `npm run build`
# inside the container. CI only escapes this because a fresh runner checkout has
# no node_modules -- on a developer machine it fails every time. Do not delete
# these lines.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Backend host build output, same reasoning: backend/build/ and backend/.gradle/
# exist on the host and are worthless (and large) inside the context.
# `.gradle` is a distinct path component from the wrapper directory `gradle`,
# which the backend build still needs, so `gradle/` is deliberately not ignored.
build
**/build
.gradle
**/.gradle

# --- Environment files (was frontend/.dockerignore) ---------------------------
# This is a security control, not a build-speed one. The build context is
# separate from git: an untracked frontend/.env.local would still enter the
# build stage, and Vite inlines any VITE_-prefixed value into the shipped client
# bundle. Losing this rule is a silent secret leak, not a build failure.
# (This also excludes the tracked root .env.example; no Dockerfile copies it.)
.env*
**/.env*

# --- Repo directories that must not enter a root context ----------------------
# .claude holds two *registered git worktrees* under .claude/worktrees/, each a
# full copy of this repo (~282 MB total today). Without this exclusion the
# context balloons and both builds slow to a crawl. This is the single most
# important line in the file for build time.
.claude
.superpowers
.git

# IDE / toolchain state. Unlike the three entries above, these exist at the repo
# root AND at depth (backend/.idea and backend/.kotlin both exist on this host),
# so they get the same bare + `**/` pair treatment as the build-output group.
.idea
**/.idea
.kotlin
**/.kotlin
